1
00:00:26,086 --> 00:00:32,086
Relax is a drama film directed by Hannah Robinson.

2
00:00:52,173 --> 00:00:58,173
Comfort and discomfort: a couple having sex on a train.

3
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:24,260
"Relax" is the debut single by English new wave band Frankie Goes to
Hollywood, released in the United Kingdom by ZTT Records in 1983.

4
00:01:44,347 --> 00:01:50,347
Produced by Trevor Horn and featuring the band along with other musicians, entered the UK Top 75
singles chart in November 1983 but did not crack the Top 40 until early January 1984.

5
00:02:10,434 --> 00:02:16,434
Three weeks later it reached number one, on the chart dated 28
January 1984, replacing Paul McCartney's "Pipes of Peace".

6
00:02:36,521 --> 00:02:42,521
One of the decade's most controversial and most commercially successful records, "Relax" eventually sold a reported
two million copies in the UK alone, easily ranking among the ten biggest-selling singles in the UK.

7
00:03:02,608 --> 00:03:08,608
It remained in the UK Top 40 for 37 consecutive weeks, 35 of which overlapped with a
radio airplay ban by the BBC (owing to lyrics perceived as overtly sexual).

8
00:03:28,695 --> 00:03:34,695
In June 1984, bolstered by the instant massive success of the band's follow-up single "Two Tribes",
the single re-entered the Top Ten for a further nine weeks, including two spent at no.

9
00:03:54,782 --> 00:04:00,782
2 (behind "Two Tribes").

10
00:04:20,869 --> 00:04:26,869
At that time, Frankie Goes to Hollywood were the only act apart from the Beatles and
John Lennon to concurrently occupy the top two positions on the chart.

11
00:04:46,956 --> 00:04:52,956
Several 12-inch single versions (and the "Frankie Say Relax"
T-shirt craze) further fed the "Relax" phenomenon.

12
00:05:13,043 --> 00:05:19,043
The single re-entered the UK Top 75 in February 1985 and, more successfully,
in October 1993, when it spent three weeks in the Top Ten.

13
00:05:39,130 --> 00:05:45,130
"Relax" remains the UK's biggest-selling debut single of all time.

14
00:06:05,217 --> 00:06:11,217
In the United States "Relax" was also comparatively
slow in reaching its chart peak.

15
00:06:31,304 --> 00:06:37,304
Released in March 1984, albeit with a different mix and nearly a
minute shorter in length, the single stalled at no.

16
00:06:57,391 --> 00:07:03,391
67 on Billboard's Hot 100 in May during a seven-week run, but it ranked number one for the year
on the Los Angeles "alternative rock" station KROQ, as voted for by listeners.

17
00:07:23,478 --> 00:07:29,478
In January 1985, a release of "Relax" that was far more similar
to the UK hit version entered the Hot 100 at no.

18
00:07:49,565 --> 00:07:55,565
70, and in March it reached no.

19
00:08:15,652 --> 00:08:21,652
10 during its 16-week run.

20
00:08:41,739 --> 00:08:47,739
In January 1989, the single was certified gold by the RIAA.

21
00:09:07,826 --> 00:09:13,826
In February 1985, the record was awarded Best British Single of 1984 at the Brit
Awards, and Frankie Goes to Hollywood won Best British Newcomer.

22
00:09:33,913 --> 00:09:39,913
A version of the song features on Frankie Goes to Hollywood's debut album
Welcome to the Pleasuredome, released in October 1984.
